{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789547943,"nonce":"N7KBzdKf109VuQpiw9Ob8jaZfhE=","title":"Nested loop inside generator iteration produces empty lists","message":"A loop nested inside iteration over a generator can silently skip its body.\n\n    xs = ([x, x + 1] for x in [1, 2])\n    print([[y for y in ys] for ys in xs])\n\nCPython prints `[[1, 2], [2, 3]]`; zypy prints `[[], []]`. The same result occurs with explicit nested `for` loops. Replacing the outer generator with a list gives the correct result.\n\nThis blocks the unmodified meteor-contest kernel: `get_puzzle()` produces ten empty piece lists instead of lists with 12 permutations (six for one piece). `get_footprints()` then exits with signal 11, so the kernel has no valid correctness or speed verdict. With the module-global collision fixed in commit 193e07f, this is the next observed blocker.\n\nA pre-optimization HIR dump for the minimal reproducer calls `zb_any_iter` for the inner sequence but does not use its result to execute the inner loop. The exact lowering stage that removes it is not established.","files":null}]}